{
 "id": "015-fener-alayinda-adim-adim",
 "lang": "en",
 "title": "Step by Step in the Lantern Parade",
 "theme": "Republic Day and walking together",
 "blurb": "On the evening of Republic Day, Zeynep and Can become the lantern keepers. When Can's steps get muddled in the parade, an outstretched hand and the beat of the drum put everything right.",
 "voiceNames": {
  "zeynep": "Zeynep",
  "can": "Can",
  "baba": "Dad",
  "ela": "Ela (the girl next door)"
 },
 "pages": [
  {
   "text": "Today is the 29th of October, Republic Day! The flags in the street went flutter, flutter! Dad explained: “Ataturk founded our Republic. Tonight we'll celebrate with a lantern parade!” Zeynep and Can jumped up and down: hop hop!",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Hooray, it's the holiday!"
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "Lantern parade, lantern parade!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Happy Republic Day!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the flag!",
   "ask": "Have you ever seen a street decorated with flags? What was there?",
   "cues": [
    {
     "w": 14,
     "fx": "yaprak"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "It got dark. Dad lined up five lanterns. “Who are the lantern keepers?” he asked. “We are!” said Zeynep and Can. Each lantern had a tiny battery light inside. Press the button: click!",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "We'll light them!"
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "I'm a lantern keeper too!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Well done, my lantern keepers!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "say": "Click! The light is on.",
    "done": "Five lanterns shining bright!",
    "msg": "The lanterns are lit!"
   },
   "hint": "Touch the lanterns to light them!",
   "ask": "Which job would you like to be in charge of at home?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Boom boom boom! Dad beat the drum, and the parade set off. Can's feet got muddled: right, left, left, right! His lantern spun round and round. Can fell behind and got in a flap. His heart went thump, thump.",
   "hits": {
    "can": {
     "say": "My heart's going thump, thump! Wait for me!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Boom boom, off we go, parade!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "My lantern is shining bright!"
    }
   },
   "predict": {
    "q": "What do you think Can will do?",
    "res": "Both are good ideas! Let's see.",
    "opts": [
     {
      "l": "He holds Zeynep's hand"
     },
     {
      "l": "He listens to the drum"
     }
    ]
   },
   "hint": "Touch Can!",
   "ask": "Why did Can get in a flap? What do you do when you get in a flap?",
   "cues": [
    {
     "w": 37,
     "fx": "kalp"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Zeynep stopped and held out her hand: “Hold my hand, Can! Listen to the drum.” Can took a deep breath. Boom, one! Boom, two! Boom, three! Boom, four! Step by step, Can came closer.",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Hold my hand, Can!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Boom… boom… nice and slow."
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "say": "Boom! One more step.",
    "done": "Can caught up! They held hands.",
    "msg": "He caught up!"
   },
   "hint": "Touch Can so he takes a step!",
   "ask": "How did Zeynep help Can?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Now their steps were as one. Boom, right! Boom, left! The neighbours joined in, and so did Zeynep's friend Ela! The lanterns swung this way and that. All together: “One step you, one step me!”",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "One step you!"
    },
    "can": {
     "say": "One step me!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Boom, right! Boom, left!"
    },
    "ela": {
     "say": "I'm coming too!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the drum first!",
   "ask": "“One step you, one step…” Can you say the end?",
   "mech": {
    "done": "One step you, one step me!",
    "msg": "The steps are as one!"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "The parade stopped in front of the big flag. Everyone lifted their lantern high. “Happy birthday, Republic!” they said. Can put his lantern down and hugged Zeynep. “With you, my steps came together!” The dark street glowed with lanterns.",
   "hits": {
    "can": {
     "say": "With you, my steps came together!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Happy birthday, Republic!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "Well done to my lantern keepers!"
    },
    "ela": {
     "say": "My lantern is up high!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the flag!",
   "ask": "What does your family do on holidays? How do you celebrate?"
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "OB6 Civic Literacy: realises that Republic Day is a day celebrated together as a community; takes on a role in the holiday event in their neighbourhood.",
   "HSAB.6 Showing movement patterns in harmony with a partner or group: walks hand in hand with a friend, step by step, following the beat of the drum (\"Boom, one! Boom, two! Boom, three! Boom, four!\").",
   "TADB.3 Analysing what they hear or watch: retells the events of the story (lighting the lanterns, falling behind, walking hand in hand) in the order they happened.",
   "SDB2.2 Cooperation and E2.2 Responsibility: becomes a lantern keeper and holds out a hand when a friend falls behind; lives the values of D19 Patriotism and D16 Responsibility in an everyday holiday experience."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show 29 October on the calendar: “Today is the Republic's birthday. Ataturk founded our Republic.”",
   "Look at the cover: “What might these children be getting ready for? What can you see in the street?”",
   "Make a “boom, boom” rhythm by tapping on the table or a tambourine; the child counts steps on the spot."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 2, wait for the child to light the lanterns: say “You're a lantern keeper too!” Stress that the lanterns run on batteries; there is no real flame.",
   "On page 3, touch Can so he says “My heart's going thump, thump!” Ask “How does our heart beat when we get in a flap?” For the prediction question, praise both options; they both work.",
   "On page 4, with every touch say “Boom, one! Boom, two! Boom, three! Boom, four!” together and tap your knees.",
   "On page 5, touch the drum first, then Zeynep, then Can; say the chant “One step you, one step me!” back and forth. Point out the neighbours' lanterns on the pavement behind."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Why did Can get in a flap? What happened in his body when he did? (His heart went thump, thump.) What helped him?",
   "What idea did Zeynep have? What would you do for a friend who fell behind?",
   "The lantern parade is a holiday tradition. How do families celebrate the holiday? Match with picture cards (hanging flags, lantern parade, holiday visits); the child tells about their own family's tradition."
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Class Lantern Parade",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "Coloured paper, glue, sticks, battery LED candles (no real candles or matches), small paper flags, a tambourine or drum",
   "adim": [
    "Make lanterns out of paper; the teacher places a battery LED candle inside each one.",
    "Each child presses the button on their lantern and becomes a “lantern keeper.”",
    "Walk hand in hand in pairs to the beat of the tambourine: “Boom, one! Boom, two!”",
    "Slow the beat down and speed it up; partners match their steps to each other, and wait for a partner who falls behind.",
    "Make a circle in front of the flag, say “Happy birthday, Republic!” and share how everyone feels."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Who lit the lanterns? How did they light them?",
   "How did Can feel when he fell behind? Who helped him?",
   "How did Zeynep and Can bring their steps together?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Lantern parade at home",
   "d": "Make a lantern out of paper or a jar and put a battery LED light inside. Walk hand in hand down the hallway to the beat of a saucepan “drum”: “One step you, one step me!” Then talk about your family's holiday traditions."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Step by Step in the Lantern Parade.” Zeynep and Can became the lantern keepers on Republic Day; when Can fell behind in the parade he got in a flap, and he found his steps again with Zeynep's outstretched hand and the beat of the drum. At home you can talk together about 29 October and your family's holiday traditions. If you make a lantern, please use a battery light instead of a real candle."
 },
